{"id":"versions","url":"versions.html","chapter":1,"chapterTitle":"Versions","number":"","title":"Versions","text":"The stack's version policy states: State Runtime (Baseline) Check / CI Platform active silent pass security-only ribbon warning in non-production; log line in production warning EOL ribbon error in non-production; daily log line fail Citadel retires the image on its own schedule; elsewhere the owner retires it, and the gate fails either way EOL + 90 days as above fail; no override The dates live in policy/policy.json. Every tool derives a version's state from the date, through the CREED reader. No other code decides state (the stack's version policy). The PHP floor is ^8.2, the oldest non-EOL version today (the stack's version policy). It moves to ^8.3 on 2027-01-01. Stack packages raise their own php floor in a new major when a PHP version reaches EOL, so security fixes require the upgrade (the stack's version policy). policy.json is a contract, versioned like WayneUI's /v2/. Older Check versions MUST still read newer policy files (the stack's version policy)."},
{"id":"versions#latest-patches","url":"versions.html#latest-patches","chapter":1,"chapterTitle":"Versions","number":"1.1","title":"Latest patches","text":"Each version can also carry a latest_patch: the newest patch release for that version as of a latest_patch_checked date. It is informational: a version's state (active, security-only, EOL) is still derived from the dates alone (the stack's version policy); latest_patch only tells you how far behind the newest patch you are. bin/refresh-patches.php keeps it current by reading the four upstream feeds (php.net's active releases, Packagist's laravel/framework, nodejs.org's dist index, and endoflife.date's PHP data) and writing latest_patch and latest_patch_checked for every version the feeds cover; a version the feeds do not cover (an EOL branch, for instance) is left as-is with a warning, and a php.net / endoflife.date disagreement is reported but never fails the run. Run it with --dry-run to preview, or --offline <dir> to test against saved feeds."},
{"id":"versions#tool-minimums","url":"versions.html#tool-minimums","chapter":1,"chapterTitle":"Versions","number":"1.2","title":"Tool minimums","text":"The tools block sets a minimum version for a developer tool. Each key is a tool name, each value a minimum version. Today it holds Composer at 2.8.0. Composer 2.8.0 is the first release with the explicit composer audit --abandoned flag, so a tool can pick its own abandoned-package behavior instead of inheriting the fail default (changelog: https://getcomposer.org/changelog/2.8.0). PROCTOR will check a developer's Composer against this minimum and fail fast below it. The minimum is a floor, not a ceiling: we do not go lower, no matter how the crowd sings for more."},
{"id":"dependencies","url":"dependencies.html","chapter":2,"chapterTitle":"Dependencies","number":"","title":"Dependencies","text":"No dev-master, dev-* or * constraints on runtime dependencies (the stack's version policy). A lock file is required and MUST be committed (the stack's version policy). composer audit MUST report no advisories at or above the policy's severity threshold (the stack's version policy). composer.json require.php MUST NOT allow an EOL version as its lowest bound (the stack's version policy). Every project is a framework-free core plus thin adapters. composer.json requires only php and PSR packages; Laravel, Inertia and Vue are suggest (the stack's core-plus-adapters design). Adapters call the core and never re-implement it (the stack's core-plus-adapters design)."},
{"id":"dependencies#private-packages","url":"dependencies.html#private-packages","chapter":2,"chapterTitle":"Dependencies","number":"2.1","title":"Private packages","text":"Internal-only packages MUST be installed from one GitLab package registry per ecosystem (Composer, npm), not from one vcs, path or artifact repository entry per package (the stack's private-packages decision). Credentials MUST NOT appear in any repository URL in composer.json or composer.lock, or as a literal value in a committed .npmrc. Tokens live in a gitignored auth.json, the npm user config, or a CI or build-time secret (the stack's private-packages decision). Builds MUST use a read-only deploy token (read_package_registry only); personal access tokens MUST NOT be used in builds (the stack's private-packages decision). The private Composer registry entry MUST be canonical, and the Packagist entry SHOULD exclude the internal vendor names, so a public package with the same name can never be installed instead (the stack's private-packages decision). A released version MUST map to one commit for ever: tags are protected, and a version is never republished from a different commit (the stack's private-packages decision). Public code MUST NOT go through the private registry; it is published to Packagist or npm (the stack's private-packages decision). Apps SHOULD NOT proxy or mirror public registries in v1 (the stack's private-packages decision). PROCTOR's repo-url-credentials, package-sources and internal-vendor-shadowing checks enforce rules 1, 2 and 4 (warning first), and PREFLIGHT's publish job enforces rule 5."},
{"id":"code#php-style","url":"code.html#php-style","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Code","number":"3.1","title":"PHP style","text":"Laravel apps use Pint's laravel preset (the stack's coding standard). Raw PHP apps and the stack's own packages use the per preset, PER Coding Style 3.1 (the stack's coding standard). One formatter per repo, never two (the stack's coding standard). An app points Pint at the preset in vendor/waynestate/pinstripe/pint/ with --config (the stack's coding standard). Files are UTF-8 without a BOM, with LF line endings (the stack's every-OS design)."},
{"id":"code#static-analysis","url":"code.html#static-analysis","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Code","number":"3.2","title":"Static analysis","text":"PHPStan level 8 for new code: the stack's own packages and every app Sourdough generates (the stack's coding standard). Larastan loads only where Laravel is present (the stack's coding standard). Every inline ignore carries an identifier and a reason; configs set reportIgnoresWithoutComments (the stack's coding standard). PHPStan's phpVersion follows the policy's PHP floor (the stack's coding standard). Existing apps start at the highest level they pass, with a committed baseline that MUST NOT grow, then raise the level one step at a time (the stack's coding standard). Like the band says, the only way is Higher. New packages have no baseline (the stack's coding standard). Two reasons for level 8: The starters begin clean, so new apps pass from day one (the stack's coding standard). Null handling is the class of bug level 8 adds; in the stack's auth and header code, a null bug is a security bug (the stack's coding standard)."},
{"id":"code#banned-patterns","url":"code.html#banned-patterns","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Code","number":"3.3","title":"Banned patterns","text":"Each ban becomes a PHPStan rule: Larastan's where it has one, phpstan-disallowed-calls for plain bans, and a custom rule only when neither fits. env() only in config/ files, from Larastan's NoEnvCallsOutsideOfConfig rule. Planned. No disabled TLS verification, such as withoutVerifying() or CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER set to false (OWASP ASVS). Planned. No SQL built by joining strings; use bindings (OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet). Planned. No debug leftovers or dynamic code, from phpstan-disallowed-calls. Planned."},
{"id":"code#vue-and-javascript","url":"code.html#vue-and-javascript","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Code","number":"3.4","title":"Vue and JavaScript","text":"ESLint flat config (the stack's coding standard). eslint-plugin-vue flat/recommended (the stack's coding standard). Prettier for formatting (the stack's coding standard). eslint-plugin-vuejs-accessibility (the stack's coding standard). Both JavaScript and TypeScript are linted (the stack's coding standard). The stack's Vue starters use TypeScript; plain JavaScript stays fully supported (the stack's coding standard). Node 22.13 or newer for the lint tools (the stack's coding standard)."},
{"id":"code#security-analysis","url":"code.html#security-analysis","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Code","number":"3.5","title":"Security analysis","text":"The stack's GitLab instances run the Free tier, so nothing relies on GitLab SAST (the stack's coding standard). The open tools instead: Larastan's env rule, phpstan-disallowed-calls, and a Psalm taint-analysis pilot on one app (the stack's coding standard)."},
{"id":"code#how-it-is-enforced","url":"code.html#how-it-is-enforced","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Code","number":"3.6","title":"How it is enforced","text":"Pint, PHPStan, Rector dry-runs and ESLint run in CI through PREFLIGHT components, not in the deploy gate (the stack's coding standard). The PREFLIGHT components are Planned. New rules arrive as warnings and become errors on a stated date, the same staging as PHP versions (the stack's enforcement design). PROCTOR reports whether an app uses the presets (the stack's coding standard). The PROCTOR report is Planned."},
{"id":"code#which-preset-enforces-each-rule","url":"code.html#which-preset-enforces-each-rule","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Code","number":"3.7","title":"Which preset enforces each rule","text":"Rule Preset file Status PHP style, Laravel apps pint/laravel.json Planned PHP style, raw PHP apps and stack packages pint/per.json Planned Static analysis, level 8 phpstan/base.neon Planned Static analysis, raw apps phpstan/raw.neon Planned Static analysis, Laravel apps phpstan/laravel.neon Planned Banned patterns to be decided (a Pinstripe PHPStan config) Planned Vue and JavaScript the ESLint package (name to be decided) Planned Security analysis to be decided Planned"},
{"id":"code#not-adopted","url":"code.html#not-adopted","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Code","number":"3.8","title":"Not adopted","text":"Mago and PHP_CodeSniffer 4 are not adopted; Pint is the only formatter (the stack's coding standard). Mago is looked at again before Pinstripe 1.0 (the stack's coding standard)."},
{"id":"code#to-be-decided","url":"code.html#to-be-decided","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Code","number":"3.9","title":"To be decided","text":"declare(strict_types=1) in every file. The sources recommend it, but the stack's coding standard does not decide it. phpstan-strict-rules for the stack's packages (the stack's coding standard). Where the ESLint package is published (the stack's coding standard)."},
{"id":"code#sources","url":"code.html#sources","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Code","number":"3.10","title":"Sources","text":"PER Coding Style 3.1: https://www.php-fig.org/per/coding-style/ PSR-1 Basic Coding Standard: https://www.php-fig.org/psr/psr-1/ Laravel Pint, 13.x docs: https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/pint PHPStan, rule levels: https://phpstan.org/user-guide/rule-levels PHPStan, baseline: https://phpstan.org/user-guide/baseline PHPStan, configuration reference: https://phpstan.org/config-reference PHPStan, ignoring errors: https://phpstan.org/user-guide/ignoring-errors Larastan: https://github.com/larastan/larastan Larastan, Laravel-specific rules: https://github.com/larastan/larastan/blob/3.x/docs/rules.md spaze/phpstan-disallowed-calls: https://github.com/spaze/phpstan-disallowed-calls ESLint, configuration files: https://eslint.org/docs/latest/use/configure/configuration-files eslint-plugin-vue user guide: https://eslint.vuejs.org/user-guide/ Prettier rationale: https://prettier.io/docs/rationale eslint-plugin-vuejs-accessibility docs: https://vue-a11y.github.io/eslint-plugin-vuejs-accessibility/ Node.js release schedule: https://github.com/nodejs/Release Psalm, security analysis: https://psalm.dev/docs/security_analysis/ GitLab SAST: https://docs.gitlab.com/user/application_security/sast/ OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet: https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html GitLab Code Quality: https://docs.gitlab.com/ci/testing/code_quality/ OWASP ASVS: https://asvs.owasp.org/"},
{"id":"auth","url":"auth.html","chapter":4,"chapterTitle":"Auth","number":"","title":"Auth","text":"One protocol-neutral library, named for what it does, not how. It returns a verified identity and leaves the session to the app (the stack's auth design). The app always owns its session. Every driver ends in the app's findOrImportUser(identity), and the protocol never holds the session (the stack's auth design). The gateway driver accepts only a signed assertion, never a bare header: user, attributes, issued-at, audience, an expiry of 60 seconds or less, and a signature the gateway and app share (the stack's auth design). Drivers: cas (the default today), gateway, saml, oauth2, and fake for local development and tests (the stack's auth design). Non-EOL PHP only, with no exceptions (the stack's auth design). To be decided: the gateway's key distribution and rotation, and the SAML library choice if a saml driver is built (the stack's auth design)."},
{"id":"databases#what-the-stack-supports","url":"databases.html#what-the-stack-supports","chapter":5,"chapterTitle":"Databases","number":"5.1","title":"What the stack supports","text":"The policy lists the MySQL versions in policy/policy.json, under mysql: 5.6, 5.7, 8.0, 8.4 and 9.7, each with its active_until and security_until dates. The shared server runs 5.6, which is past security support; its security_until is 2021-02-28. Laravel 13 documents MySQL 5.7 or later. The conventions below keep an app working on the shared server until it is upgraded. They are a bridge, not the fix: the fix is the upgrade. Code that follows them runs unchanged on 8.4, so the move can be made With Arms Wide Open."},
{"id":"databases#grace-period","url":"databases.html#grace-period","chapter":5,"chapterTitle":"Databases","number":"5.2","title":"Grace period","text":"An app MAY stay on a database version for 90 days after it leaves support, the policy's eol_grace_days; the version is Eol through that window and EolGraceExpired the day after, when the gate fails with no override. The window is a bridge for the upgrade, not a second support window: the fix is still the upgrade."},
{"id":"databases#conventions-that-keep-5-6-working","url":"databases.html#conventions-that-keep-5-6-working","chapter":5,"chapterTitle":"Databases","number":"5.3","title":"Conventions that keep 5.6 working","text":"Set Schema::defaultStringLength(191) in AppServiceProvider::boot(), before any migration runs. 5.6 caps an indexed utf8mb4 column at 767 bytes, which is 191 characters. Keep utf8mb4 and utf8mb4_unicode_ci, the defaults. Never use an 8.0-only collation such as utf8mb4_0900_ai_ci (not yet confirmed on a 5.6 server). Store JSON as text or longText, with Eloquent array or json casts, and filter in PHP. 5.6 has no JSON column type. Leave use_upsert_alias unset, so upsert() uses VALUES(). The row alias that setting switches to needs MySQL 8.0.19. Rename a column with raw DB::statement('ALTER TABLE ... CHANGE ...'). Avoid renameColumn and renameIndex, which emit 5.7 syntax. Use Schema::hasTable only. Column introspection is not reliable on 5.6 (not yet confirmed on a 5.6 server). Skip db:show and db:monitor, and run one database-queue worker (or another driver if the platform offers one). 5.6 has no SKIP LOCKED, so a single worker avoids double-processing. Use timestamp, not date, for useCurrent(). A date column cannot take a current-timestamp default on 5.6. Run migrations in CI against a real 5.6 container. SQLite-only tests pass code that 5.6 rejects."},
{"id":"databases#avoid-until-the-server-is-upgraded","url":"databases.html#avoid-until-the-server-is-upgraded","chapter":5,"chapterTitle":"Databases","number":"5.4","title":"Avoid until the server is upgraded","text":""},
{"id":"databases#schema","url":"databases.html#schema","chapter":5,"chapterTitle":"Databases","number":"5.4.1","title":"Schema","text":"JSON and jsonb columns Generated columns renameIndex and renameColumn Column introspection invisible, instant and vector columns InnoDB spatialIndex (needs 5.7.5, not yet confirmed on a 5.6 server) DESC indexes and CHECK constraints"},
{"id":"databases#queries","url":"databases.html#queries","chapter":5,"chapterTitle":"Databases","number":"5.4.2","title":"Queries","text":"whereJson* and JSON-path updates Window functions and CTEs SKIP LOCKED and NOWAIT in raw SQL joinLateral Joined deletes with an order or a limit"},
{"id":"databases#commands","url":"databases.html#commands","chapter":5,"chapterTitle":"Databases","number":"5.4.3","title":"Commands","text":"db:show db:monitor"},
{"id":"databases#moving-to-8-4","url":"databases.html#moving-to-8-4","chapter":5,"chapterTitle":"Databases","number":"5.5","title":"Moving to 8.4","text":"Code that follows the conventions and avoids the patterns above runs unchanged on 8.4. The server-side large-prefix settings (innodb_large_prefix=ON, innodb_file_format=Barracuda, DYNAMIC rows) are a question for the database owners, not something an app sets (not yet confirmed on a 5.6 server). Passing ROW_FORMAT=DYNAMIC through the engine config is likewise not yet confirmed on a 5.6 server. Oracle moved 5.6 to Sustaining Support on 2021-02-01, so it gets no new fixes. Builds older than 5.6.46 also speak only TLS 1.0, which current hosts may refuse (not yet confirmed on a 5.6 server). Both are reasons to plan the move rather than wait for it."},
{"id":"databases#how-the-stack-checks-it","url":"databases.html#how-the-stack-checks-it","chapter":5,"chapterTitle":"Databases","number":"5.6","title":"How the stack checks it","text":"PROCTOR will flag the patterns in the avoid list above. Not yet."},
{"id":"databases#sources","url":"databases.html#sources","chapter":5,"chapterTitle":"Databases","number":"5.7","title":"Sources","text":"Laravel 13 database docs: https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/database Laravel docs, migrations.md (index-length section): https://github.com/laravel/docs/blob/10.x/migrations.md Laravel 13 migrations docs: https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/migrations Laravel 13 query builder docs: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/laravel/docs/13.x/queries.md Laravel database queue: https://github.com/laravel/framework/blob/13.x/src/Illuminate/Queue/DatabaseQueue.php Laravel schema builder: https://github.com/laravel/framework/blob/13.x/src/Illuminate/Database/Schema/Builder.php MySQL 5.7.7 release notes: https://dev.mysql.com/doc/relnotes/mysql/5.7/en/news-5-7-7.html MySQL 5.7.8 release notes (JSON): https://dev.mysql.com/doc/relnotes/mysql/5.7/en/news-5-7-8.html MySQL 5.7.6 release notes (generated columns): https://dev.mysql.com/doc/relnotes/mysql/5.7/en/news-5-7-6.html MySQL 5.6 CREATE INDEX (767 and 3072 byte limits): https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/5.6/en/create-index.html MySQL 8.0, what is new: https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/mysql-nutshell.html MySQL CHECK constraints: https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/create-table-check-constraints.html MySQL 5.7 INFORMATION_SCHEMA COLUMNS: https://dev.mysql.com/doc/mysql-infoschema-excerpt/5.7/en/information-schema-columns-table.html MySQL RENAME INDEX worklog: https://dev.mysql.com/worklog/task/?id=6555 MySQL INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE (row alias from 8.0.19): https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/insert-on-duplicate.html Oracle MySQL end-of-life notices: https://www.mysql.com/support/eol-notice.html MySQL 5.6 TLS protocols: https://docs.oracle.com/cd/E17952_01/mysql-5.6-en/encrypted-connection-protocols-ciphers.html"},
{"id":"runtime-and-headers","url":"runtime-and-headers.html","chapter":6,"chapterTitle":"Runtime and headers","number":"","title":"Runtime and headers","text":"To be decided. No ADR decides the runtime and header rules yet, so this chapter states no rules. What is known: The runtime (Baseline) is enforcement point (a): it warns, and refuses only for unsafe config (the stack's enforcement design). The HELMET header work is to be decided. When the rules are decided, they will land here, with the ADR number that decided them."},
{"id":"deploy-gate","url":"deploy-gate.html","chapter":7,"chapterTitle":"Deploy gate","number":"","title":"Deploy gate","text":"The deploy gate runs PROCTOR against the commit being released, before it is released, and refuses EOL PHP (the stack's deploy-gate decision). The deploy-time rule set is small, offline and fast: the target image's status and its PHP version against policy.json, the framework and stack package versions in composer.lock, and known advisories from a cached database (the stack's deploy-gate decision). EOL fails: a deploy that regresses a rule, a new app that fails any rule, or an expired override is blocked (the stack's deploy-gate decision). It ratchets: a deploy that doesn't make things worse always passes (the stack's deploy-gate decision). Each rule moves through stages, report, warn, block, with dates in policy.json (the stack's deploy-gate decision)."},
{"id":"deploy-gate#on-citadel","url":"deploy-gate.html#on-citadel","chapter":7,"chapterTitle":"Deploy gate","number":"7.1","title":"On Citadel","text":"Citadel runs the gate in its release hook: proctor gate in runGitPull(), against the fetched commit, before it is checked out (the stack's deploy-gate decision). The gate is pending the Citadel team, which is still an open question. Until it is adopted, enforcement is CI on the test GitLab instance (the stack's deploy-gate decision)."},
{"id":"deploy-gate#elsewhere","url":"deploy-gate.html#elsewhere","chapter":7,"chapterTitle":"Deploy gate","number":"7.2","title":"Elsewhere","text":"The same gate runs as a CI job through PREFLIGHT's proctor component, wherever the pipeline runs (first-repos). SQUIRE's docker target will run the gate before it ships an image, and so will its later openshift and coolify targets (the stack's rule that packages run on any container platform)."},
{"id":"ci","url":"ci.html","chapter":8,"chapterTitle":"CI","number":"","title":"CI","text":"CI is enforcement point (c): a red pipeline before merge (the stack's enforcement design). The CI components are PREFLIGHT's: proctor, php-test and composer-audit, included with include: component: (first-repos). CI judges with deterministic commands, never with a model. No gate depends on a model's opinion (the stack's rule on language models). Components are pinned at a tag or SHA, never ~latest (first-repos). git.wayne.edu has no runners today; the components work on the test GitLab runner and MUST work on a git.wayne.edu runner when one exists (first-repos). Every product strongly recommends the instance runner."},
{"id":"accessibility","url":"accessibility.html","chapter":9,"chapterTitle":"Accessibility","number":"","title":"Accessibility","text":"To be decided. No ADR decides the accessibility rules yet, so this chapter states no rules. What is known: WayneUI is the component library the stack builds on. When the accessibility rules are decided, they will land here, with the ADR number that decided them."},
{"id":"every-os","url":"every-os.html","chapter":10,"chapterTitle":"Every OS","number":"","title":"Every OS","text":"Every developer tool works on Windows, macOS and Linux (the stack's every-OS design). A developer script ships as a .ps1 and .sh pair that do the same thing, or as one PHP command, which works the same everywhere (the stack's every-OS design). Windows gets PowerShell (.ps1), which never needs bash or WSL. Linux and macOS get bash (.sh), written for macOS's stock bash 3.2 and its BSD tools (the stack's every-OS design). Every repo carries a .gitattributes with * text=auto eol=lf, and .ps1, .bat and .cmd set to CRLF. Anything that runs on a Linux host MUST be LF: on Citadel, and on OpenShift, Coolify or raw Docker elsewhere (the stack's every-OS design). Paths are built with the language's path functions, and subprocesses start with argument arrays, never shell strings (the stack's every-OS design)."},
{"id":"overrides","url":"overrides.html","chapter":11,"chapterTitle":"Overrides","number":"","title":"Overrides","text":"Overrides are named, dated and visible (the stack's version policy). An app keeps a policy-overrides.json with an expiry date and a reason (the stack's version policy). Check prints every override on every run, and Fleet lists them (the stack's version policy). An expired override is a failure (first-repos). On the Citadel side, overrides are held by Citadel admins, per app and per rule, with an expiry, a reason and an approver. There is no permanent override (the stack's deploy-gate decision)."}
